    "description": "Edit INSIDE a nest, by nest id — get_project lists them under nests, and a nest clip's source.project names the one it shows. With it, every id in this call — clips, tracks, actions — is looked up in THAT nest's own tracks and clips instead of the timeline's, and every time is the nest's own, measured from its zero (a nest has no speed, so the whole mapping is t minus the nest clip's start); media ids are unaffected, because a nest shares the document's media. The op itself is unchanged and one call is still one undo step. Omit it to edit whatever the app has OPEN — the timeline, or the nest set_scope entered, exactly as an edit made by hand goes where the window is looking — so this argument is how you act on a container you are not looking at, and get_project always says which one you are in. Eight ops have no meaning inside a nest and refuse by name when you ask for one: add_marker, set_marker, remove_marker, set_canvas, add_media, relink_media, remove_media, add_adjustment_clip. The first seven behave DIFFERENTLY when the scope is inherited from set_scope rather than asked for here, and the result says which: five of them, plus set_marker's name / chapter / color and remove_marker, simply land on the DOCUMENT and are named under document_ops. The two that carry a TIME — add_marker, and set_marker with a time — are refused instead, because a marker is at project time while the playhead inside a nest is not, so the tick you would send means the wrong frame. Leave the nest (set_scope with no arguments) or pass the tick in project time. And read the result rather than assuming it landed: an edit can be perfectly valid inside the nest and still be refused for the whole DOCUMENT's sake, since the clip and track caps and the 8-deep nesting limit are counted across every container at once.",
